# Overfitting

Creating models that are too complex for the problem, capturing noise rather than signal. Overfitting leads to models that perform well on historical data but fail on new data.

## Key Ideas

- Models that fit every data point perfectly often fail to generalize
- Simpler models frequently outperform complex ones
- Compare models against naive baselines to detect overfitting
- Calibration plots and backtesting help identify overfit models
- Related to [[signal-to-noise-ratio]] and [[model-selection]]

## Connection to Silver

Silver emphasizes that many prediction failures in finance and economics stem from overfitted models that captured noise during stable periods.